-- Native golden (Phase 7.2, Signal.watch Stage B): a watched USR2 delivered by
-- a self-raise must run its handler exactly once, from the scheduler drain, on
-- a normal stack.  The handler prints AFTER main's body because dispatch is
-- deferred: that ordering is the ONLY thing distinguishing a deferred drain
-- from a handler run synchronously inside Signal.raise, so it is the property
-- this golden exists to pin.  Do not "simplify" it into an order-free check.
--
-- ORDERING HAZARD — this rule must run at MARCH_NUM_SCHEDULERS=1.
-- march_signal_drain() is called at the TOP OF EVERY sched_loop iteration on
-- EVERY scheduler thread (runtime/march_scheduler.c).  At the default (one
-- scheduler per CPU; it was a flat 4 when this was measured) the other
-- threads are idle-spinning in that loop, so the
-- self-raise's pending flag is claimed by whichever thread gets there first —
-- typically a worker, microseconds later, running the handler CONCURRENTLY
-- with main's own println.  That made this golden fail ~4% of the time under
-- load, two ways: `caught usr2` landing before `after raise`, and the two
-- threads' writes tearing into one line (`after raisecaught usr2`).  Measured
-- 8/200 at the default vs 0/200 at MARCH_NUM_SCHEDULERS=1 on the same binary.
-- See specs/progress/2026-08-21-signal-watch-output-ordering-flake.md.
--
-- At one scheduler nothing else can run while main's green thread runs, so the
-- drain cannot happen until main finishes — the ordering is then a genuine
-- guarantee rather than a typical outcome.  native_signal_watch_multisched
-- keeps the >1-scheduler drain covered, with an assertion that does not depend
-- on an interleaving the runtime never promised.
mod Main do
  needs IO.Console
  needs IO.Signal
  fn main(_cap_console : Cap(IO.Console), _cap_signal : Cap(IO.Signal)) do
    Signal.watch(Signal.Usr2, fn -> println("caught usr2"))
    println("before raise")
    Signal.raise(Signal.Usr2)
    println("after raise")
  end
end
